[Music]  it was a Thursday morning  I just taken the last few sips of my  coffee now lukewarm from sitting out too  long  I sent off the last email and was about  to start in on the real work for the day  but first decided to take a quick break  and check Facebook so I open up Facebook  and amidst an endless stream of recent  engagement photos and birthdays I saw a  photo that was very different from the  rest you might have seen it to do with a  photo of a tiny Syrian boy his limp body  washed up on the shore of Turkey now up  until this point for me the Syrian  refugee crisis had just been a news  story  it was stories of deaths and bombings  and things completely unrelated to my  life with this photo change that this  photo put a face to the conflict there  is a three-year-old boy named Alan Kurdi  who had died because of this conflict  now I'm not an expert on Syria I'm not  someone who dedicated my recent life's  work to bringing relief to this conflict  I'm like most of you here I'm someone  who hears the statistics 12 million  people have rooted from their homes  500,000 people dead I hear those stats  what I still find it incredibly  difficult to empathize in a meaningful  way and that's the problem today our  empathy for humanitarian crises is out  of shape and lazy it's time to Train it  we can build a daily routine to train  our empathy from sleeping to waking to  action just like a morning routine our  empathy routine begins in a trance  unaware of the world around us  I call this the empathy sleepwalks next  we must rouse our emotion to wake up  from that sleep walk and I call this the  empathy alarm clock and finally one for  awake we must take action throughout the  day this is empathy exercise we are all  in an empathy sleepwalk at some point or  another there's a saying one death is a  tragedy one million is a statistic when  we hear the death tolls in Syria 500,000  people it's just an abstract number that  number passes in one ear and out the  other just as if we were sleepwalking  research support this idea that we don't  work well in the abstract in a recent  study based on the Iraq war participants  were told there were either 15  casualties in a battle or 500 but in  both cases participants demonstrated  zero difference in emotional response  between the two the abstract nature of  those numbers walls participants into a  sleepwalk unable to perceive difference  in magnitude or weight now being in this  sleep walk in and of itself is not a bad  thing I start our empathy routine here  because sometimes we need to rest and  recharge in order for our empathy to be  most effective sometimes we need an  escape from the endless statistics and  abstract numbers that modern media sends  our way but it's not okay to stay in the  sleep block state forever just as with  every morning routine we must be able to  wake up and face the day so how do we do  that how do we set off the alarm clock  to startle us awake let's go back to  that research study well there is no  difference between the 15 and 500 when  those numbers are accompanied by images  of the fallen soldiers  the emotional response spiked  dramatically for the 500 this highlights  the concepts at concrete images not the  abstract numbers they represent can wake  up our emotion we said earlier that the  500,000 deaths in Syria feels abstract  so now let's make that concrete to wake  all of us up here what if I were to tell  you that number would be the same as if  everyone in this room and all of our  classmates outside and every alumni  that's ever graduated from the GSB  dating back to the skooled inception was  decimated and now multiply that by 15  the challenge before us is to seek out  and create these alarm clock moments I  ask you to speak out independent news  sources that move beyond those abstract  numbers as we just did check out the  Syrian campaign for another opportunity  to wake up your emotion and then share  those moments to create the alarm clocks  for those around you the retweet of a  photojournalism article or the  screenshot of your donation to a relief  organization like the white helmet or  the low-key notes that you give to your  peers on the crisis these are the  concrete moments or the alarm clocks if  you will that can wake up the empathy of  an entire community once our empathy is  awake the last step is to exercise it  empathy is like a muscle for many of us  our empathy may be like our deltoids  it's kind of this random shoulder muscle  that you don't engage often and to  exercise that you really have to go out  of your way but the goal is to exercise  our empathy like our quadriceps they're  constantly engaged for standing and  walking and you don't have to think  about it it's second nature  we can learn this lesson from another  refugee crisis where modest exercise of  empathy muscles had tremendous lasting  impact during the Vietnam War there's an  American family called the trip priests  in Long Island they were a middle-class  family of 11  the father retired fireman turned  security guard now with 11 mouths to  feed you might think that the trip  region has that much extra to give but  they exercise our empathy every day  so when Vietnamese refugees began  arriving in America the trip frees  empathy muscles were primed to help as  easily as walking they opened up their  home to a young family of three refugees  the trip reach ildren never complained  about doubling or even tripling up in  their bedrooms they didn't think twice  about how they fit 14 people around an  already overflowing dinner table each  night they just huddled together with  smiling faces and open arms to share  their love with complete strangers  empathy for them was muscle memory  the trip fries are a beautiful example  of the ripple effect that empathy  exercise can have I know because the  family of three refugees that they saved  with my own family here's my brother and  my mom and dad it's now 40 years later  and were again confronted with a tragic  refugee crisis what have we learned the  trip we taught us that we have to  exercise our empathy every day even in  small ways to make this a part of your  routine pair with an action you already  take when you call your family to check  in a luxury many Syrians don't have take  two extra minutes to call your  representatives to influence their  stance on US refugee policy or when you  pay your monthly gym bill set aside 10%  to donate to a relief organization like  the International Rescue Committee I'm  soon with this daily training our  empathy muscles can do the heavy lifting  without breaking a sweat  and the idea of tackling something as  immense as Syria or as impossible as  sitting 14 people around a tiny dinner  table no longer will seem that daunting  I want to leave you with one thought  it's that we can train our empathy to be  a powerful tool follow this routine from  resting with the empathy sleep walk wake  it up with an empathy alarm clock and  then strengthen it with small actions  every day and put the exercise we sleep  we wake we exercise so the next time  you're confronted with a heartbreaking  photo of a little boy you'll be ready to  help because it's now just a part of  your routine  [Applause]  [Music]

